course: biology
lesson: 381
title: Silkworms and goldfish: two animals China changed
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In a school box, small white caterpillars munch mulberry leaves so loudly that the tray sounds like soft rain. They are silkworms, and people in China have kept them for thousands of years.
explain: Farmers always chose the calmest worms that made the best silk, generation after generation, until the silk moth could no longer live without people: it cannot fly properly and is not found anywhere in the wild.
explain: Goldfish have a similar story. Long ago people in China noticed rare golden fish among dull grey-green wild carp, kept the brightest in ponds and let them have babies, until the colours grew strong.
explain: Later breeders chose odd shapes too, such as bubble eyes and double tails. These shapes help no fish in the wild; they exist only because people like how they look.
explain: Changing an animal by choosing which ones have babies is called breeding, and it shows how much living things can change over many generations.
fact: Silkworms are the caterpillars of the domestic silk moth, and they eat almost nothing but fresh mulberry leaves.
fact: The domestic silk moth cannot fly properly and no longer lives in the wild; it depends on people completely.
fact: A silkworm has six true legs at the front and ten soft prolegs with tiny hooks further back; the moth keeps only the six true legs.
fact: A silkworm grows thousands of times heavier in about a month and sheds its skin four times, because old skin cannot stretch.
fact: A silkworm spins a cocoon from one single thread of liquid silk over two or three days, and the thread can be longer than five football pitches.
fact: The story of an empress whose tea cup caught a cocoon is a legend, but traces of silk in old Chinese graves are reported to be about 8,500 years old.
fact: For a long time making silk was a secret, and carrying silkworm eggs out of China was forbidden.
fact: Silk is made of protein, like hair, and its smooth fibres shine and feel soft; to get the long thread most cocoons are heated, so the pupa inside dies.
fact: Goldfish come from a wild carp of East Asia, and people in China kept rare golden ones more than a thousand years ago.
fact: Fancy goldfish with bubble eyes, puffy heads or double tails were bred by people, and some swim slowly or see badly.
fact: A wild goldfish has one tail fin; a fantail has a doubled tail, two tail fins side by side joined at the front.
fact: Goldfish dumped in lakes can grow as big as a football, eat other fishes' eggs, stir up mud and crowd out native fish, and over generations they often turn dull again, partly because birds spot and catch the bright ones first.
exercise: What do silkworms eat? | Almost only fresh mulberry leaves | Any green grass they find in the field around the farm | Mostly cabbage and lettuce from the garden | Small insects and flies they catch
exercise: What is a silkworm, really? | The caterpillar of the silk moth | A kind of earthworm that spins silk | A baby snake that spins a silky nest | A beetle larva living deep in the soil under trees
exercise: Why could domestic silk moths not survive in the wild? | They cannot fly properly and depend on people | They are too big to hide | They only eat chocolate | They cannot breathe outside a box, so they die in fresh air
exercise: How many true legs does a silkworm have? | Six | Ten | Sixteen | Eight
exercise: What are the ten soft stumps further back on a silkworm? | Prolegs with tiny hooks for gripping | Extra true legs, like the front ones | Little wings growing out of its back | Breathing tubes
exercise: How many times does a silkworm shed its skin as it grows? | Four times | Never at all | Once every day | Only once, at the start
exercise: Why must a growing caterpillar change its skin? | Its old skin cannot stretch any more | Its skin gets dirty from all the leaves it eats | It is far too hot inside the old skin | Birds keep biting off bits of the old skin
exercise: What is a silk cocoon made of? | One single very long thread | Hundreds of short hairs glued together | Chewed mulberry leaves pressed flat | Wet mud mixed with its own spit
exercise: What is true about the empress and the tea cup story? | It is a legend, but silk really began in China long ago | It was filmed on a camera and proves that silk is new | It happened in France last century | It is the reason silk is blue
exercise: Why did China keep silk-making secret? | Silk was precious, and the secret made it valuable to sell | Silkworms are dangerous to touch, so nobody else was allowed near | Other countries hated silk | The worms were sacred and never used
exercise: What is silk made of? | Protein, the same kind of stuff as hair | Plastic made in a factory | Plant fibres like cotton, from the mulberry tree | Tiny bits of metal
exercise: What fish did goldfish come from? | A dull grey-green wild carp of East Asia | A bright orange sea fish from the coral reefs | A small shark from Japan | A river salmon from Canada
exercise: How did goldfish get their bright colours? | People bred the brightest fish over many generations | They ate orange food for years | The hot sun dyed them in shallow ponds for many years | They were painted by monks
exercise: Why do fancy goldfish exist at all? | People chose and bred them for their looks | They are better at hiding in the wild among the weeds of rivers | They evolved in deep caves | They came from another planet
exercise: How many tail fins does a fantail goldfish have? | Two, side by side, joined at the front | Only one, just like a wild goldfish or carp | Four, set in a cross shape behind its body | None at all, the tail has been bred away
exercise: What happens when pet goldfish are dumped in a lake? | They can grow huge and harm the native fish | They die at once in cold water | They turn into carp the next day | They clean the lake perfectly
test: «silkworms are just worms innit, like in the garden» What is right? | No, they are caterpillars that turn into moths | Yes, they are earthworms that spin silk out of mud | Yes, they live in soil | No, they are baby snakes
test: Count them: a tray has 5 silkworms, each with 16 legs in all. How many legs is that? | 80 | 60 | 30 | 50
test: «ok so when it turns into a moth how many legs does it lose» Answer for one silkworm. | Ten | Six | Sixteen | None
test: What would happen if a farmer let silk moths go free in a forest? | They would soon die, as they cannot fly or fend for themselves | They would spread and take over the forest | They would turn wild and grow bigger | They would fly back home every evening to the farmer's warm shed to sleep
test: In a cartoon a silkworm knits a jumper with knitting needles. What does a real silkworm do? | Spins one long thread round itself to make a cocoon | Knits silk with its front legs | Weaves cloth for people on a tiny loom of leaves | Spins webs to catch flies
test: «that cocoon's tiny, the thread must be like 2 cm» What is right? | One thread can be longer than five football pitches | Yes, it is about 2 cm long | It is made of no thread at all, only of dried hardened spit | It is as long as one ruler
test: «my bro says silk comes from a plant like cotton» What is right? | No, silk is a protein spun by the silkworm | Yes, from the bark of the mulberry tree that silkworms eat | Yes, from cotton flowers | No, it comes from sheep
test: Count them: 3 wild goldfish and 2 fantails swim in a pond. How many tail fins in all? | Seven | Five | Ten | Six
test: «gonna tip my goldfish in the lake, it'll be happier there» What is the best answer? | No, it can harm wild fish; ask a pet shop or rehome it | Yes, goldfish belong in lakes | Yes, but only in winter | Fine, it will turn back into a wild carp in a week or two
test: Why are dumped goldfish often dull-coloured again after a few generations in a lake? | Bright fish are easier for birds to catch, so dull ones survive | The lake water slowly washes the bright colour off their scales | Fish lose colour when they are happy | They eat too many plants
test: «bubble-eye goldfish must be the best swimmers, look at em» What is right? | No, many fancy kinds swim slowly or see badly | Yes, the bubbles help them float and swim much faster | Yes, they live wild in rivers | No, they cannot swim at all
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the domestic silk moth. | It is incapable of proper flight and wholly dependent upon human beings | It is a vigorous flier abundant in the wild forests of Asia and Europe | It subsists principally upon insects | It constructs its cocoon from mud and leaves
exam: Ascertain the aggregate number of true legs and prolegs possessed by a single silkworm. | Sixteen | Six | Ten | Eight
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the colours of the goldfish were intensified over the centuries. | Selective breeding by human keepers of the brightest individuals | Exposure to strong sunlight in shallow ponds | A diet consisting exclusively of orange foodstuffs supplied by monks | A sudden change in the climate of East Asia
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequences of releasing goldfish into natural waters? | They may attain great size and harm native fish populations | They invariably perish within a single day | They purify the water to the benefit of all species | They transform into a different species forthwith
